# Does a fur coat's mode-M deficit scale with STRAND DENSITY, at the density the creature uses?
#
# SUPERSEDES the first draft of this scene, which had two flaws found by reading fur_creature.ftsl
# properly rather than by looking at the numbers:
#
#   1. IT COMPARED THE WRONG ROW. fur_creature's `coat`, `belly` and `tan` are all FUR materials --
#      `belly` is the pale underside COAT (`on "chest" material belly`), not skin. The rig's
#      corresponding material is therefore `hair`, the strands, NOT `p3`, the quad beneath them.
#      The first draft read p3 at -2.45 % and compared it against belly at -32 %.
#   2. `hair` WAS ONE MATERIAL ACROSS ALL THREE PATCHES, so its ROI averaged three densities into
#      one number and could not show a trend at all. Each patch now has its own strand material
#      with a byte-identical BSDF.
#
# AND THE DENSITY WAS 2.8x OFF. fur_creature authors `density 450000` (strands per unit^2) on every
# coat. The first draft used `count 160000` on a 1 m^2 patch, matched against the barrel coat's
# REALISED count over its sphere area (175 800/m^2) -- a different quantity from the authored
# density the other coats use. This sweep is authored in the same units as the creature and brackets
# its value: 50k, 150k, 450k per m^2.
#
# p0 IS THE BY-CONSTRUCTION NULL, unchanged and already known to pass at +0.13 %: no fur at all, so
# mode M and mode R must agree on it for any reason whatsoever. If p0 moves, nothing else can be read.
#
# PREDICTION, REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN:
#   h1 > h2 > h3 in deficit, monotone in density, and h3 (450k/m^2, the creature's own value) lands
#   in the -17 % to -33 % range fur_creature's coats show. If h3 is instead a few percent like the
#   first draft's -2.29 %, then density is NOT the missing factor and the difference lies in the
#   geometry the coats sit on -- spheres, not a flat quad -- which is the next thing to vary.
scene { units meters  spectral 360 830 1 }

# One BSDF per role, several names. Any difference between patches is strand density, never material.
material "p0" { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.75 }
material "p1" { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.75 }
material "p2" { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.75 }
material "p3" { type diffuse  reflect whitewall 0.75 }
material "h1" { type diffuse  reflect rgb 0.42 0.26 0.13 }
material "h2" { type diffuse  reflect rgb 0.42 0.26 0.13 }
material "h3" { type diffuse  reflect rgb 0.42 0.26 0.13 }

quad "q0" { origin 0.0 0 0   u 0.5 0 0   v 0 0 0.5   material p0 }
quad "q1" { origin 0.7 0 0   u 0.5 0 0   v 0 0 0.5   material p1 }
quad "q2" { origin 1.4 0 0   u 0.5 0 0   v 0 0 0.5   material p2 }
quad "q3" { origin 2.1 0 0   u 0.5 0 0   v 0 0 0.5   material p3 }

# Authored in the creature's own units. Identical grooms; ONLY `density` differs.
fur "f1" { on "q1"  material h1  density 50000   length 0.05  radius 0.0008  jitter 0.15  seed 1 }
fur "f2" { on "q2"  material h2  density 150000  length 0.05  radius 0.0008  jitter 0.15  seed 1 }
fur "f3" { on "q3"  material h3  density 450000  length 0.05  radius 0.0008  jitter 0.15  seed 1 }

# An env light: no geometry to stand between the camera and the patches, and the broadest source
# available, which is what a thin fiber needs.
light env { spd preset:d65 }

camera "cam" {
    eye 1.3 4 0.25   look_at 1.3 0 0.25   up 0 0 1   fov_y 45
    mode M
    film { res 256 256 }
}
